      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Back pain is a frequent and often ominous clinical sign in patients with ductal pancreatic cancer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From 1971 to 1993 a pancreatic carcinoma could be resected in 192 patients, whereas 261 patients underwent either probatory laparotomy alone or palliative bypass procedures. In a retrospective study including uni- and multi-variate survival analysis we have determined the impact of preoperative back pain on both resectability and long-term prognosis after resection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the presenting symptoms of patients with ductal pancreatic cancer back pain was a predictive sign of irresectability. In the presence of preoperative back pain the long-term prognosis after resection of the tumour was also significantly impaired. In a multivariate analysis it could be demonstrated that the prognostic impact of back pain was as strong as the influence of residual tumour, tumour grading, and tumour size."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Back pain often indicates irresectability of ductal pancreatic carcinoma and also impairs the long-term prognosis even after curative resection."
        }
      ]},
